  "worked_solution": "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the left, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(1,-4),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x+2)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(1,-4).$\n\n- Then, we translate the graph by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1-2,-4)=(-1,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-1\\cdot (-4))=(-1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,4+4)=(-1,8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ is $(-1,8).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
